title: Backslashes in attribute values in spec v2
summary: "Pins how spec v2 reads quoted attribute values: a backslash before the value's own quote escapes it as in 1.10, two backslashes are one, and every other backslash stays as written. attribute-escapes.smd pins the same cases read with spec v1."
status: draft
updated: 2026-10-17
---

## Escaped quotes

:::note{title="It's \"done\""}
A title with both kinds of quote, written as `smd fmt` writes it.
:::

Activation :metric[42%]{label="The \"active\" users' share" trend=up} this week.

## Escaped backslashes

:::note{title="C:\docs\\"}
A value that ends in a backslash: the last one is written `\\`, the one before an ordinary letter is not.
:::

:::note{title="\\\\server\share"}
Two backslashes in a row are written `\\\\`: the title is a UNC path with two leading backslashes.
:::

Saved to :metric[3]{label="C:\temp\\" trend=up} today.

## Backslashes that are not escapes

:::note{title="C:\Users\docs\plan.smd"}
A Windows path keeps its backslashes.
:::

:::note{title='say \"hi\"'}
Inside single quotes `\"` is two characters, as in spec v1.
:::

## A value that ends in one backslash

Valid in spec v1, which reads it without escapes; in spec v2 the list doesn't parse, so the line is text.

